   "stale_delta": [],
   "fact_conflicts": [
    "g10.r1.scope: g10.r1.g10.r1.s1.l1 says 'it cant, actually. the ledger resets to zero each minute' \u2014 Stated as how the tracker works: the amount gone over by is \"gone. its not sitting anywhere when the next minute opens\". A reader concludes there is no negative accounting at all, so no -25000.0 / -250 / -125 floor exists to land on. \u2014 and nothing later overturns it",
    "g10.r1.scope: g10.r1.rev1 says 'so tokens can be underwater while requests is fine, and neither one goes deeper than that fraction of its own limit' \u2014 Read as an aside about which axes the floor applies to, it puts the request axis under the same -0.25 * limit floor as tokens, so a reader expects available_request_capacity to bottom out at -0.25 * max_requests_per_minute. The decision is that available_request_capacity is never floored at all, and the per-axis floors are the input/output token axes under `seperate`. Nothing later corrects this: say19 only explains the None case, and s2.l4 leaves an unfloored -50 unexplained. \u2014 and nothing later overturns it",
    "g10.r2.rule: g10.r2.s1_l4 says 'a request that actually reached the provider is spent for that minute ... so an error response still counts, even with nothing usable in it ... whatever came back, yes. it went out, it does not un-happen' \u2014 A reader takes away that a request which reached the provider and came back with an error keeps its charge for that minute -- no slot and no budget handed back, with only never-sent requests refunded. The decision is that the failure path calls refund_capacity(blocked), which returns the whole blocked token estimate and adds exactly 1.0 to available_request_capacity regardless of what the errored response reported. Nothing later names this rule and retires it; s2_l3, s2_l4 and rev1 state the opposite behaviour without referring back to it, so a reader seeing both cannot tell which is current. \u2014 and nothing later overturns it",
    "g10.r2.scope: g10.r2.s1_l4 says 'a request that actually reached the provider is spent for that minute ... so an error response still counts, even with nothing usable in it ... the line is whether it left us, not what came back' \u2014 This answers the failure-refund question directly and answers it the wrong way: an attempt that went out and came back an error keeps its slot charged against the minute, so the except-Exception path would end at 59.0 rather than 60.0. Konrad's closing 'not what the code does today though' invites the reader to treat today's refund as the thing to remove. The decision is that every terminal path through the except branch refunds, leaving available_request_capacity == 60.0 and available_token_capacity == 10000.0. Nothing later names this left-us/came-back rule and retires it -- rev1 states the opposite outcome but grounds it in 'nothing went out', so the two read as being about different situations. \u2014 and nothing later overturns it"
   ]
  }
